      <div class="kpi-value">1980s</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Most Diverse Decade</div>
      <div class="kpi-detail">10 unique winners in 10 ceremonies</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Section 1: Overview -->
  <section id="overview">
    <div class="section-num">Section I</div>
    <h2>Dataset Overview &amp; Historical Context</h2>
    <p>The Academy Award for Best Original Score has been presented at every ceremony since 1934, making it one of the longest-running categories in Oscar history. Over 93 ceremonies, the award has recognized 69 distinct composer entries for scoring 92 unique films&mdash;a body of work that traces the evolution of film music from the orchestral grandeur of early Hollywood through electronic experimentation and into today's hybrid soundscapes.</p>
    <p>The dataset spans from Louis Silvers' win for <em>One Night of Love</em> (1934) to Ludwig G&ouml;ransson's win for <em>Sinners</em> (2026). Across these nine decades, the category has undergone name changes and occasional splits (e.g., separate dramatic and comedy/musical score awards in certain years), but this analysis consolidates to one winner per ceremony year.</p>

    <div class="note-box">
      <strong>Data note:</strong> The dataset lists "Oppenheimer" as the winning film for both 2023 and 2024, with Ludwig G&ouml;ransson as composer in both years. This appears to be a data entry anomaly (the 2024 ceremony's actual Best Original Score winner may differ). This analysis reports the data as provided, but the reader should note this potential discrepancy when interpreting G&ouml;ransson's win count.
