  <!-- Section 4: Era-Based Analysis -->
  <section id="era-analysis">
    <div class="section-num">Section IV</div>
    <h2>Era-Based Analysis of Winning Scores</h2>
    <p>Grouping the data into four broad historical eras reveals how the character of Oscar-winning scores&mdash;and the profiles of their composers&mdash;have transformed alongside the film industry itself.</p>

    <div class="era-grid">
      <div class="era-card">
        <h3>The Golden Age</h3>
        <div class="era-years">1934&ndash;1960 &middot; 27 ceremonies</div>
        <div class="era-stat"><strong>20</strong> unique composers &middot; <strong>7</strong> repeat wins</div>
        <p>The era of the great studio orchestrators. Max Steiner, Dimitri Tiomkin, Alfred Newman, Mikl&oacute;s R&oacute;zsa, and Franz Waxman defined a lush, symphonic idiom that became synonymous with Hollywood itself. Steiner set the early template with 3 wins, while Tiomkin's 3 wins across the 1950s cemented the dominance of European-trained orchestral composers. The Golden Age's repeat-winner rate (26%) established a pattern of recognizable names cycling through the award.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="era-card">
        <h3>The Silver Age</h3>
        <div class="era-years">1961&ndash;1980 &middot; 20 ceremonies</div>
        <div class="era-stat"><strong>17</strong> unique composers &middot; <strong>6</strong> repeat wins</div>
        <p>A transitional era that saw the orchestral tradition evolve as pop, jazz, and world music influences entered Oscar-winning scores. Maurice Jarre bridged old and new with epic, culturally inflected scores for <em>Lawrence of Arabia</em> and <em>Doctor Zhivago</em>. John Barry brought a suave sophistication, while John Williams' late-decade arrival with <em>Jaws</em> (1975) and <em>Star Wars</em> (1977) signaled a neo-romantic revival. Notably, this era also recognized singular stylistic departures: Burt Bacharach's pop sensibility (1969) and Giorgio Moroder's electronic score (1978).</p>
      </div>
      <div class="era-card">
        <h3>The Modern Era</h3>
        <div class="era-years">1981&ndash;2000 &middot; 20 ceremonies</div>
        <div class="era-stat"><strong>16</strong> unique composers &middot; <strong>7</strong> repeat wins</div>
        <p>The Modern Era is defined by its stylistic breadth. The 1980s produced perfect diversity (10 unique winners), while the 1990s saw Alan Menken's Disney dominance (3 wins in 4 years) and John Williams' continued relevance with <em>Schindler's List</em> (1993). The era also brought international voices to prominence&mdash;Ryuichi Sakamoto, Luis Bacalov, Nicola Piovani&mdash;and crowned Hans Zimmer for <em>The Lion King</em> (1994), beginning a career that would span decades.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="era-card">
        <h3>The Contemporary Era</h3>
        <div class="era-years">2001&ndash;2026 &middot; 26 ceremonies</div>
        <div class="era-stat"><strong>20</strong> unique composers &middot; <strong>10</strong> repeat wins</div>
        <p>The contemporary period has been marked by globalization, genre diversity, and the rise of a new dominant voice. Howard Shore's <em>Lord of the Rings</em> wins bookended the early 2000s; Gustavo Santaolalla brought Latin American minimalism; A.R. Rahman won for <em>Slumdog Millionaire</em>. Trent Reznor &amp; Atticus Ross introduced an electronic/ambient aesthetic. But it is Ludwig G&ouml;ransson who has emerged as the era's defining figure, with 4 wins by age 42&mdash;the fastest accumulation in category history. Hildur Gu&eth;nad&oacute;ttir's win for <em>Joker</em> (2019) was a landmark as one of the very few solo female winners in the category's history.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="chart-container">
      <div class="chart-title">Composer Concentration by Era: Repeat Win Rate</div>
      <canvas id="eraChart"></canvas>
    </div>
    <p>The Contemporary Era shows the highest repeat-win rate at 38.5%, driven significantly by G&ouml;ransson's four wins. While the Golden Age's repeat rate was elevated by longer career spans and a smaller composer pool, the Contemporary Era's concentration is notable because it occurs despite a vastly larger and more globalized talent field.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 5: Notable Milestones -->
  <section id="notable-milestones">
    <div class="section-num">Section V</div>
    <h2>Notable Milestones &amp; Records</h2>

    <ul class="milestone-list">
      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">1934</span>
        <strong>The First Winner:</strong> Louis Silvers won the inaugural Best Original Score award for <em>One Night of Love</em>, establishing a tradition that would span nine decades.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">1950&ndash;51</span>
        <strong>Consecutive Wins:</strong> Franz Waxman became one of the first composers to win back-to-back, with <em>Sunset Boulevard</em> (1950) and <em>A Place in the Sun</em> (1951). Alan Menken later matched this feat with wins from 1989&ndash;1992 (three consecutive years), and Gustavo Santaolalla repeated it in 2005&ndash;2006.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">1975&ndash;77</span>
        <strong>The Williams Era Begins:</strong> John Williams won for <em>Jaws</em> (1975) and <em>Star Wars</em> (1977), launching the most iconic composer-franchise partnership in film history and inaugurating the neo-romantic blockbuster score.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">1989&ndash;92</span>
        <strong>Menken's Disney Streak:</strong> Alan Menken won 3 times in 4 years (<em>The Little Mermaid</em>, <em>Beauty and the Beast</em>, <em>Aladdin</em>), dominating the late Golden Age of Disney Animation.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">1994 &amp; 2021</span>
        <strong>Longest Gap Between Wins:</strong> Hans Zimmer holds the record for the longest span between wins&mdash;27 years separated <em>The Lion King</em> (1994) from <em>Dune</em> (2021), a testament to extraordinary career longevity.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">2015</span>
        <strong>A Legend's Overdue Honor:</strong> Ennio Morricone, one of cinema's most celebrated composers, won his first (and only) competitive Oscar for <em>The Hateful Eight</em> at the age of 87.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">2019</span>
        <strong>Breaking Barriers:</strong> Hildur Gu&eth;nad&oacute;ttir won for <em>Joker</em>, becoming one of the very few women to win the Best Original Score Oscar in its 93-year history.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">2018&ndash;2026</span>
        <strong>Fastest to Four Wins:</strong> Ludwig G&ouml;ransson reached four wins in just eight years (<em>Black Panther</em>, <em>Oppenheimer</em> &times;2, <em>Sinners</em>)&mdash;the shortest span to accumulate four wins in the category's history.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">2026</span>
        <strong>Most Recent Winner:</strong> Ludwig G&ouml;ransson won his fourth Best Original Score Oscar for <em>Sinners</em> at the 98th Academy Awards ceremony.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 6: Conclusions -->
  <section id="conclusions">
    <div class="section-num">Section VI</div>
    <h2>Key Takeaways &amp; Forward-Looking Observations</h2>

    <div class="takeaway-grid">
      <div class="takeaway-item">
        <div class="takeaway-num">1</div>
        <div>
          <p><strong>Dominance is cyclical, not constant.</strong> Every era produces one or two defining voices (Steiner, Tiomkin, Williams, G&ouml;ransson), but periods of concentration are reliably followed by surges in diversity. The 1950s' Tiomkin dominance gave way to the eclectic 1960s; Williams' 1970s&ndash;80s reign preceded the perfectly diverse 1980s. This suggests the current G&ouml;ransson era will likely yield to a more distributed landscape.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="takeaway-item">
        <div class="takeaway-num">2</div>
        <div>
          <p><strong>The talent pool has globalized dramatically.</strong> The Golden Age was dominated by European &eacute;migr&eacute;s (Steiner, Tiomkin, R&oacute;zsa, Korngold) who brought Central European orchestral traditions. The Contemporary Era has recognized composers from India (Rahman), Argentina (Santaolalla), China (Tan Dun), Iceland (Gu&eth;nad&oacute;ttir), Sweden (G&ouml;ransson), and France (Desplat, Bource), reflecting the industry's increasingly global character.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="takeaway-item">
        <div class="takeaway-num">3</div>
        <div>
          <p><strong>The 1980s remain an anomaly worth studying.</strong> With a perfect 100% diversity rate (10 unique winners in 10 ceremonies), the 1980s represent a singular moment of openness in the category. This coincided with a period of genre experimentation (jazz, electronic, world music influences) that the Academy proved willing to reward. No other decade has matched this level of variety.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="takeaway-item">
        <div class="takeaway-num">4</div>
        <div>
          <p><strong>Repeat winners accumulate faster now.</strong> G&ouml;ransson's four wins in eight years contrasts with Barry's 24-year span and Williams' 18-year span to reach the same count. Whether this reflects changing Academy dynamics, franchise-driven filmmaking, or individual brilliance, the pace of accumulation has visibly accelerated.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="takeaway-item">
        <div class="takeaway-num">5</div>
        <div>
          <p><strong>Looking ahead to the late 2020s:</strong> the category faces an interesting tension between the gravitational pull of established names (G&ouml;ransson, Zimmer, Desplat) and the Academy's demonstrated willingness to recognize emerging and diverse voices. If historical patterns hold, we should expect fresh names to break through in the coming years, potentially from regions and traditions not yet represented in the winner's circle.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

